      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "An experimental autoimmune model was used to study effects of nerve growth factor deprivation on the peptide content of sensory neurones and on pain sensitivity. Rats exposed during development to anti-nerve growth factor antibodies showed an increased threshold towards nociceptive stimuli. These animals also showed a significant decrease in substance P levels in their dorsal root ganglia and spinal cord. These results suggest that substance P-containing primary sensory neurones are involved in certain types of nociception."},
